  "Bundle/Commerce order refund and dispute tracking (revenueTotalUsd exclusion, pipeline-health + digest visibility, Commerce payouts panel clawback flag) is visibility- and prevention-only — there is no automated clawback (money recovery from a client who was already paid out stays a manual operator conversation). Commerce Buy now DOES have a Mark-paid-out rejection UI: markCommerceOrderPaidOutAction (src/app/dashboard/leads/actions.ts) rejects the action once order.refundedAt/disputedAt is set, and leads-client.tsx swaps the button for 'Refunded/Disputed — nothing owed' (or '— claw back from client' once already paid out). Bundle orders have no such UI to reject in the first place, since that ledger (orders table) is never mechanically disbursed via a dashboard action at all — see /docs/architecture for the full distinction. Any new dispute/refund lifecycle event needs both its 'opened' and 'closed/reversed' twin shipped in the same pass (see refund.updated / clearRefunded, commerce-order-refund-reversal-tracking), or the flag gets stuck open after Stripe reinstates funds. A reversal/closed-twin handler keyed on a single sub-object EVENT's own status (e.g. refund.updated's per-refund status=failed/canceled) is not ground truth by itself when the parent object can have multiple independent sub-objects — Stripe allows several partial refunds on one charge, so a second refund failing must not blindly clear refundedAt if a different, earlier refund on that same charge genuinely succeeded. Re-fetch the parent (stripe.charges.retrieve) and re-check its own aggregate field (chargeHasRefund's amount_refunded, the same predicate charge.refunded already uses) before clearing — never trust the single event's status alone (refund-reversal-charge-aggregate-recheck).",
  "Any customer-facing notice tied to a paused/failed billing state (e.g. invoice.payment_failed's sendPaymentFailedNotice) needs its restore/recovered twin shipped alongside it — same class as the dispute/refund opened/closed rule above, but for outbound email rather than a dashboard flag. sendPaymentRecoveredNotice (invoice.paid) only fires when clearOrdersRecurringCanceledByCustomer actually cleared a prior pause (cleared > 0) — an ordinary healthy renewal's invoice.paid must stay silent, or every subscriber gets a spurious 'service resumed' email every billing cycle (payment-recovered-confirmation-notice). pause_collection is a distinct axis from invoice.payment_failed/invoice.paid — it fires no invoice event at all, so those two notices never see it. customer.subscription.updated's handler sends sendSubscriptionPausedNotice/sendSubscriptionResumedNotice (deliberately separate copy from the payment-failed pair — 'your card was declined' is false when collection was paused, not a failed charge) gated on Stripe's own event.data.previous_attributes actually containing a `pause_collection` key for this specific event (not merely 'pause_collection is currently set/unset') — this both stops re-notifying on every unrelated subsequent subscription.updated while a pause persists AND stops double-emailing an ordinary past_due/unpaid pause/restore that the invoice.* pair already covers (subscription-pause-collection-notice).",

